WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.160
Kiln runs open models in your JVM.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.270
For business, industrial,
medical and creative work,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:10.986
or anywhere a specialized
model earns its keep.

00:00:10.986 --> 00:00:13.333
Plain Java calls, on your GPU.

00:00:13.887 --> 00:00:18.403
If you want a local language model
or embeddings in Java, there are options.

00:00:18.403 --> 00:00:21.909
They work, though they're often
slower than they should be.

00:00:21.909 --> 00:00:25.652
But what if you want to flag
a fracture on an X-ray for review?

00:00:25.652 --> 00:00:27.672
Spot defects on a production line?

00:00:27.672 --> 00:00:29.157
Read a stack of invoices?

00:00:29.157 --> 00:00:31.058
Isolate the vocals from a track?

00:00:31.058 --> 00:00:33.613
Or any of a plethora
of other capabilities?

00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:35.990
Until now, from the JVM, you were stuck.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:40.601
It meant a Python stack, or a tool like
ComfyUI behind HTTP: another runtime,

00:00:40.601 --> 00:00:43.536
another process,
and a network hop on every call.

00:00:43.536 --> 00:00:45.853
Kiln calls the native engines directly.

00:00:45.853 --> 00:00:48.289
No Python, no server, nothing in between.

00:00:48.854 --> 00:00:51.894
It uses Java's Foreign
Function and Memory API,

00:00:51.894 --> 00:00:55.193
so the call into the engine
is a plain native call.

00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:58.741
Weights load once and stay
on the GPU between requests,

00:00:58.741 --> 00:01:03.837
and when memory runs short, Kiln unloads
the models you've used least recently.

00:01:03.837 --> 00:01:07.999
Text streams back token by token,
and models can call your tools.

00:01:08.554 --> 00:01:13.004
In Kiln Studio, read an invoice
into JSON, spot a defect on a part,

00:01:13.004 --> 00:01:15.594
or flag an X-ray for a doctor's review.

00:01:15.594 --> 00:01:19.278
Or chat, paint an image,
write a song, or render a clip.

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:24.531
On our test laptop, Kiln made a one-minute
song up to twice as fast as ComfyUI,

00:01:24.531 --> 00:01:25.594
on the same GPU.

00:01:26.154 --> 00:01:30.437
Pull a model from Hugging Face with
one command, and try it in Kiln Studio.

00:01:30.437 --> 00:01:33.908
Kiln needs no other Java libraries,
just its native engines,

00:01:33.908 --> 00:01:38.710
so it works in any app, and it's a great
companion for Embabel Agent: local models,

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:39.346
in process.

00:01:39.887 --> 00:01:43.060
Here's what open models
can make, running in Kiln.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.408
This is Endless Country, episode two.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:54.648
Kiln!

00:01:54.648 --> 00:01:57.652
Open models, inside your JVM!

00:01:57.652 --> 00:02:03.247
Sponsored by Rod Johnson,
and created by Jasper Blues!

00:02:03.247 --> 00:02:03.972
kiln4j.

00:02:03.972 --> 00:02:04.387
org!
